Plans (prelude/coordinator) carry one change through several designs, builds and the architecture, in order. Read its NOTES.md and RULES.md, then propose a decision for how plans fit the architecture. Discuss it with me before writing anything.

Decide with me:

which part reads plans (prelude/plans/<id>/plan.json, on the plan's own branch prelude/plan-<id>, then in main once started), works out where each step stands, and starts a ready step. Probably a new part in Designs and hosting, using branches, designs, sessions and stories;
how a step's state is worked out without being stored: from local branches, sessions, approvals and stories for this person's steps; from the remote (pushed branches, pull requests, origin's main) for other people's;
who's at this computer (git's user.name and email) and who the people are (the repo's committers);
when Prelude checks: after each turn, after a fetch, on a timer. And how a step starting on its owner's computer reaches the plans-changed event;
the planning prompt in agent: it drafts steps, assigns owners when told, rewrites prompts, and fills in what earlier steps made when a step starts. It gets the architecture brief and the visual vocabulary;
where Prelude's notes in the planning chat come from (R-d6m), and how the transcript stays merge-clean while the plan is in main.
Then write the decision, the new part and the changed parts, and a flow "A step starts".

